\title{Integer Power Laws for Contour-Integral Winding\\in Nonvanishing Optical Modes}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 18, 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
Complex optical fields carry phase singularities whose zero sets may form vortex lines, links, and knots. Around a contour avoiding all zeros, the logarithmic derivative gives an analytic measure of phase winding. We develop the multiplicative algebra of this contour integral for arbitrary smooth, nowhere-vanishing complex modes. We prove closure under multiplication and additivity of winding, establish simultaneous smoothness and winding laws for natural powers, show that inversion reverses winding, and extend the power law to every integer exponent. The culmination is a two-mode integer composition theorem: for admissible modes $\gamma,\delta$ and integers $k,\ell$, the winding of $\gamma^k\delta^\ell$ equals $k\Wind(\gamma)+\ell\Wind(\delta)$. The results isolate the analytic basis of optical charge composition without assuming winding quantization or any particular knot geometry. Numerical methods and examples illustrate the laws for helical and amplitude-modulated modes.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
A scalar monochromatic optical field is represented by a complex amplitude. Its modulus determines intensity and its argument determines phase. At a zero of the amplitude, phase is undefined. In three dimensions such zeros can trace curves, close into loops, link, and form knots. The local structure around a vortex is measured by phase circulation.

Let a complex field sampled along one azimuthal turn be $\gamma(\theta)$. If the sampling contour misses the zero set, then $\gamma$ takes values in $\C\setminus\{0\}$. The normalized logarithmic-derivative integral is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:winding-intro}
 \Wind(\gamma)=\frac{1}{2\pi i}\int_0^{2\pi}\frac{\gamma'(\theta)}{\gamma(\theta)}\,d\theta.
\end{equation}
For a periodic closed loop, a complementary quantization theorem identifies this number with an integer degree. Our concern is logically prior: the exact algebra of the integral under multiplication, inversion, and integer powers. The principal formula is
\begin{equation}\label{eq:main}
 \Wind(\gamma^k\delta^\ell)=k\Wind(\gamma)+\ell\Wind(\delta),
 \qquad k,\ell\in\Z.
\end{equation}
It applies to arbitrary continuously differentiable, nonvanishing modes, not only ideal azimuthal harmonics.

\section{Analytic setting}
Let $I=[0,2\pi]$. We define fields on $\R$ for convenience, while all winding integrals are over $I$.

\begin{definition}[Smooth nonvanishing optical loop]
A pair $(\gamma,\gamma')$ of functions $\R\to\C$ is \emph{admissible} when: (i) $\gamma$ is differentiable at every $\theta$, with derivative $\gamma'(\theta)$; (ii) $\gamma(\theta)\ne0$ for every $\theta$; and (iii) $\gamma'$ is continuous.
\end{definition}

The word ``loop'' reflects the optical application. The algebra below does not need an explicit endpoint condition $\gamma(0)=\gamma(2\pi)$; that condition is needed when interpreting winding as a topological degree.

\begin{lemma}[Logarithmic-derivative regularity]\label{lem:regular}
If $(\gamma,\gamma')$ is admissible, then
\[
 L_\gamma(\theta)=\frac{\gamma'(\theta)}{\gamma(\theta)}
\]
is continuous on $\R$ and integrable on every compact interval.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Differentiability makes $\gamma$ continuous, and $\gamma'$ is continuous by assumption. Division is continuous where its denominator is nonzero. A continuous complex-valued function is integrable on every compact interval.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}[Contour-integral winding]
For an admissible loop $\gamma$, define
\[
 \Wind(\gamma)=\frac{1}{2\pi i}\int_0^{2\pi}L_\gamma(\theta)\,d\theta.
\]
\end{definition}
The pure helical mode $\gamma_m(\theta)=e^{im\theta}$ has $L_{\gamma_m}=im$ and hence $\Wind(\gamma_m)=m$ for every $m\in\Z$.

\section{Multiplication and additivity}
The logarithmic derivative changes products into sums.

\begin{theorem}[Closure under coherent multiplication]\label{thm:mulclosure}
Let $(\gamma,\gamma')$ and $(\delta,\delta')$ be admissible. Define
\[
 \mu(\theta)=\gamma(\theta)\delta(\theta),\qquad
 \mu'(\theta)=\gamma'(\theta)\delta(\theta)+\gamma(\theta)\delta'(\theta).
\]
Then $(\mu,\mu')$ is admissible.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The product rule gives the displayed derivative. A product of nonzero complex numbers is nonzero. Since differentiable functions are continuous, each term in $\mu'$ is continuous, and so is their sum.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Logarithmic-derivative splitting]\label{lem:split}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:mulclosure},
\[
 \frac{\mu'(\theta)}{\mu(\theta)}
 =\frac{\gamma'(\theta)}{\gamma(\theta)}
 +\frac{\delta'(\theta)}{\delta(\theta)}
\]
for every $\theta\in\R$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Substitute the product-rule expression and divide by $\gamma\delta$. Both factors are nonzero, so ordinary field algebra separates the two fractions.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Winding Product Theorem]\label{thm:product}
If $\gamma$ and $\delta$ are admissible optical loops, then
\[
 \Wind(\gamma\delta)=\Wind(\gamma)+\Wind(\delta).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The product is admissible by Theorem~\ref{thm:mulclosure}. By Lemmas~\ref{lem:regular} and~\ref{lem:split}, its integrable logarithmic derivative is $L_\gamma+L_\delta$. Linearity of integration gives
\[
 \Wind(\gamma\delta)=\frac1{2\pi i}\int_0^{2\pi}(L_\gamma+L_\delta)
 =\Wind(\gamma)+\Wind(\delta).
\]
\end{proof}
This is an analytic charge-conservation law. No Fourier ansatz or constant-amplitude assumption is involved.

\section{Natural powers}
For $n\in\N$, powers are taken pointwise.

\begin{theorem}[Natural Power Theorem]\label{thm:natpower}
Let $(\gamma,\gamma')$ be admissible. For every $n\in\N$, the field $\gamma^n$ is admissible, with derivative
\[
 (\gamma^n)'=n\gamma^{n-1}\gamma'
\]
for $n>0$ and derivative zero for $n=0$. Moreover,
\[
 \Wind(\gamma^n)=n\Wind(\gamma).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Induct on $n$. The zeroth power is the constant field $1$, which is nonzero and has zero derivative and winding. If the conclusions hold for $n$, write $\gamma^{n+1}=\gamma^n\gamma$. Multiplicative closure proves admissibility; the product rule simplifies the derivative to $(n+1)\gamma^n\gamma'$. The Winding Product Theorem gives
\[
 \Wind(\gamma^{n+1})=\Wind(\gamma^n)+\Wind(\gamma)
 =(n+1)\Wind(\gamma).
\]
Thus regularity and the winding formula advance simultaneously.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Repeated composition]
The coherent product of $n$ identical admissible modes has winding $n$ times the winding of one mode.
\end{corollary}

\section{Inversion and integer powers}
Negative exponents require reciprocal fields.

\begin{theorem}[Closure under inversion]\label{thm:invclosure}
If $(\gamma,\gamma')$ is admissible, then $\gamma^{-1}$ is admissible and
\[
 (\gamma^{-1})'(\theta)=-\frac{\gamma'(\theta)}{\gamma(\theta)^2}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Nonvanishing makes the reciprocal defined and nonzero. The reciprocal rule gives the derivative. Its numerator is continuous and its denominator is continuous and never zero, so the derivative is continuous.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Winding Inversion Theorem]\label{thm:invwind}
For every admissible $\gamma$,
\[
 \Wind(\gamma^{-1})=-\Wind(\gamma).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The product $\gamma^{-1}\gamma$ is the constant field $1$. Theorem~\ref{thm:product} gives
$0=\Wind(1)=\Wind(\gamma^{-1})+\Wind(\gamma)$, and rearrangement proves the assertion.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Integer Power Theorem]\label{thm:intpower}
If $\gamma$ is admissible and $k\in\Z$, then $\gamma^k$ is admissible with a continuous derivative, and
\[
 \Wind(\gamma^k)=k\Wind(\gamma).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For $k=n\ge0$, use Theorem~\ref{thm:natpower}. For $k=-n<0$, write $\gamma^k=(\gamma^{-1})^n$. Admissibility follows from Theorems~\ref{thm:invclosure} and~\ref{thm:natpower}, while
\[
 \Wind((\gamma^{-1})^n)=n\Wind(\gamma^{-1})=-n\Wind(\gamma)=k\Wind(\gamma).
\]
The case $k=0$ is included.
\end{proof}

\section{Two-mode integer composition}
\begin{theorem}[Two-Mode Integer Composition Theorem]\label{thm:main}
Let $\gamma$ and $\delta$ be admissible optical loops and let $k,\ell\in\Z$. Then
\[
 F(\theta)=\gamma(\theta)^k\delta(\theta)^\ell
\]
is admissible, and
\[
 \Wind(F)=k\Wind(\gamma)+\ell\Wind(\delta).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The Integer Power Theorem makes both powered factors admissible. Multiplicative closure makes their product admissible. The Winding Product Theorem and the Integer Power Theorem then yield
\[
 \Wind(\gamma^k\delta^\ell)
 =\Wind(\gamma^k)+\Wind(\delta^\ell)
 =k\Wind(\gamma)+\ell\Wind(\delta).
\]
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Cancellation]
If $k\Wind(\gamma)+\ell\Wind(\delta)=0$, then $\gamma^k\delta^\ell$ has zero contour-integral winding.
\end{corollary}
Zero winding need not mean that the field is constant; it asserts only cancellation of net logarithmic circulation.

\begin{corollary}[Helical charge synthesis]
For $\gamma_p(\theta)=e^{ip\theta}$ and $\delta_q(\theta)=e^{iq\theta}$ with $p,q\in\Z$,
\[
 \Wind(\gamma_p^k\delta_q^\ell)=kp+\ell q.
\]
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Direct differentiation gives $\Wind(\gamma_p)=p$ and $\Wind(\delta_q)=q$; substitute into Theorem~\ref{thm:main}.
\end{proof}

\section{Numerical algorithms}
Suppose $\gamma$ and $\gamma'$ can be evaluated. On the uniform mesh $\theta_j=2\pi j/N$, periodic trapezoidal quadrature gives
\begin{equation}\label{eq:quad}
 \widehat{\Wind}_N(\gamma)=\frac{1}{2\pi i}\frac{2\pi}{N}
 \sum_{j=0}^{N-1}\frac{\gamma'(\theta_j)}{\gamma(\theta_j)}.
\end{equation}
It uses $O(N)$ evaluations, $O(N)$ time, and $O(1)$ auxiliary memory in streaming form. Samples with $|\gamma(\theta_j)|$ below a safety threshold must be rejected: division is ill-conditioned near zeros and may indicate that the contour crosses a singularity.

If derivatives are unavailable, one may sample principal phases, unwrap successive changes by multiples of $2\pi$, and divide the total change by $2\pi$. This also takes $O(N)$ time. It can fail if the phase rotates by more than $\pi$ between samples, so adaptive refinement is advisable.

For a composite mode, the residual
\[
 R_N=\widehat{\Wind}_N(\gamma^k\delta^\ell)
 -k\widehat{\Wind}_N(\gamma)-\ell\widehat{\Wind}_N(\delta)
\]
directly measures numerical error in the composition identity. The exact theorem predicts $R_N=0$.

\section{Examples and applications}
\subsection{Pure helices}
Take $\gamma=e^{2i\theta}$ and $\delta=e^{-3i\theta}$. With $k=4$ and $\ell=-1$,
\[
 \gamma^4\delta^{-1}=e^{11i\theta},\qquad
 \Wind(\gamma^4\delta^{-1})=4(2)+(-1)(-3)=11.
\]
The example combines amplification and reversal.

\subsection{Amplitude modulation}
Let
\[
 \gamma(\theta)=(2+0.3\cos\theta)e^{2i\theta}.
\]
The amplitude factor is positive, and
\[
 \frac{\gamma'}{\gamma}=\frac{-0.3\sin\theta}{2+0.3\cos\theta}+2i.
\]
The real term is the derivative of the logarithm of a positive periodic amplitude, so its integral over a turn vanishes. Hence $\Wind(\gamma)=2$. The theory is therefore not restricted to circles of constant modulus.

\subsection{Cancellation without triviality}
If $\Wind(\gamma)=2$ and $\Wind(\delta)=3$, then
\[
 \Wind(\gamma^3\delta^{-2})=3\cdot2-2\cdot3=0.
\]
The composite may retain nonconstant amplitude and phase; only net circulation cancels.

\subsection{Modular charge design}
If available modes have integer charges $p$ and $q$, integer-powered multiplication generates charges $kp+\ell q$. Thus all generated charges are multiples of $\gcd(p,q)$, and every such multiple is represented by suitable integers $k,\ell$. This observation combines the analytic composition law with a linear Diophantine design step. It assumes that reciprocal fields can be implemented; the theorem itself does not prescribe an optical device.

\section{Interpretation and limitations}
The operation here is pointwise multiplication, not additive interference. A product of nonzero fields remains nonzero, whereas a sum may cancel and introduce zeros on the contour. There is therefore no unrestricted counterpart asserting $\Wind(\gamma+\delta)=\Wind(\gamma)+\Wind(\delta)$.

Contour winding is local information. A small meridional contour around a three-dimensional vortex filament measures local phase charge. Knot type describes the global embedding of the entire zero curve. Filaments with distinct knot types may carry the same local charge, and winding alone does not classify a trefoil, figure-eight knot, or unknot.

The present statements also do not by themselves prove quantization. For a closed loop in $\C\setminus\{0\}$, one expects integral winding and invariance under nonvanishing homotopy. Here we establish the algebraic identities of the normalized integral without using those classification facts. This separation makes clear which conclusions follow from calculus alone.

Numerically, topological robustness does not eliminate conditioning problems. Near a zero, $\gamma'/\gamma$ can be large, fixed-grid quadrature can lose accuracy, and phase unwrapping can miss rapid rotations. Computations should report minimum sampled amplitude, refine near large logarithmic derivatives, and compare independent estimators.

\section{Future directions}
Five extensions are immediate.
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=*]
\item \textbf{Finite multimode composition.} For a finite family $\{\gamma_j\}$ and integer weights $\{k_j\}$, establish
\[
 \Wind\!\left(\prod_j\gamma_j^{k_j}\right)=\sum_jk_j\Wind(\gamma_j).
\]
\item \textbf{Homotopy invariance.} Prove that a smooth nonvanishing homotopy of closed loops has winding independent of its deformation parameter.
\item \textbf{Quantization and classification.} Show that every closed smooth nonvanishing loop has integer winding, and characterize zero winding by a periodic logarithmic lift.
\item \textbf{Singularity motion.} Model parameterized fields whose zeros trace curves in space, and prove conservation of total local winding when no boundary zero is created or destroyed.
\item \textbf{Knot-sensitive invariants.} Combine local winding with meridional and longitudinal information in explicit torus-knot models to distinguish global beam families.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}
Smooth complex optical modes that do not vanish on a sampling contour possess an exact compositional charge calculus. Multiplication preserves admissibility and adds winding; natural powers scale winding; inversion negates it; and arbitrary integer-powered products satisfy Equation~\eqref{eq:main}. The theory requires neither a constant-amplitude ansatz nor a chosen knot geometry. It supplies the analytic algebra underlying controlled optical phase charge and provides a local foundation for future work on vortex motion, quantization, and knotted singularity sets.

